wifi: mt76: mt7615: send rate sets with a 160 MHz rate at one valid bandwidth

The hardware sends all rates of a station's rate set, and a probe rate, at
one bandwidth, and mt7615 takes it from the last entry of the set. That is
harmless while the rates stay at 80 MHz or below, but not once 160 MHz
rates are in use:

 - a 160 MHz rate (or probe) goes out at 80 MHz when the last entry is an
   80 MHz rate, so statistics of 80 MHz transmissions are credited to a
   160 MHz rate;
 - rates that the hardware cannot send at 160 MHz (3 and 4 streams) go out
   at 160 MHz when the last entry is a 160 MHz rate. Those frames are lost
   without a tx status, so rate control never learns that they fail.

tx status also took the bandwidth from the requested rate, not the one used.

If a set contains a 160 MHz rate, use the bandwidth of its reference rate
(the probe rate while probing, otherwise the first rate, which is also what
decides the guard interval) for the whole set and make every VHT rate in it
valid there: at most two streams at 160 MHz, and the next lower MCS where an
MCS does not exist at that bandwidth. Duplicate removal no longer steps
such a rate below MCS 0, which turned a 2 stream MCS 0 into a 1 stream
MCS 15, or onto an MCS that does not exist. The stored set is changed in the
same way, so tx status reports what was sent. Sets without a 160 MHz rate
are handled as before.

Also declare the limit of two streams at 160 MHz to rate control, so that it
does not select such rates in the first place.

